(2000,'Instruction Function - Wiki Help','Instructions that tell the wiki engine how to process a page.','##title ##Tag(Draft)
{{{Card(Default | Table of Contents) ##toc }}}

==Overview
Instruction functions tell the wiki engine how to handle a page or its workflow. These can be things like #{@@draft, @@include, @@deprecate, etc.}#, which respectively tell the engine that the page is in draft, the page is intended to be used as an include to other pages and finally that the page needs to be deleted.


There are various other processing instructions and pages which use them can be filtered on by moderators in the admin menu.


==Functions
##TagGlossary(Instruction Function)

==Related
##related
',1,1,'12/5/2022 7:40:27 PM'),
(2001,'Scope Function - Wiki Help','Scope functions apply a function over a defined scope of wiki text.','##title ##Tag(Draft)
{{{Card(Default | Table of Contents) ##toc }}}

==Overview
Scope functions are like standard functions except for they apply to a defined scope of wiki text. This scope is defined by three opening curly-brackets and terminated by three closing curly-brackets.


{{{Code(Wiki)
#{
{{{ ThisIsTheFunction (These are function parameters)
   This is a scope body
}}}
}#
}}}

==Example
In this example, we are calling the [[Alert - Scope Function - Wiki Help|Alert()]] scope function:
{{{Code(Wiki)
#{
{{{Alert(Warning | Heading text)
   This is the body of the scope.
}}}
}#
}}}

**Output:**

{{{Alert(Warning | Heading text)
   This is the body of the scope.
}}}


==Functions
##TagGlossary(Scope Function)


==See Also
[[Function Calling Convention - Wiki Help]]
[[Scope Function - Wiki Help]]
[[Instruction Function - Wiki Help]]
[[Standard Function - Wiki Help]]

==Related
